<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 1: Warm-up =
&amp; Introduction</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Go=
od morning! I'm Sarah, a Principal Frontend Engineer here at NAB. I've been=
 with the bank for about 8 years, working primarily on our digital banking =
platform and internal tooling. Thanks for taking the time to interview with=
 us today. Could you start by giving me a brief overview of your background=
 and what drew you to apply for this Senior Frontend Engineer position?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Good=
 morning Sarah, thanks for having me! I'm excited to be here. I've been wor=
king as a frontend engineer for about 7 years now, with the last 4 years sp=
ecifically focused on React and the broader React ecosystem. Most recently,=
 I was a Senior Frontend Engineer at a fintech startup where I led the fron=
tend architecture for our core trading platform, serving over 200,000 daily=
 active users.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">What really drew me to NAB is th=
e scale and complexity of the challenges you're solving. Banking applicatio=
ns require an incredible level of reliability, security, and performance - =
and I love that intersection of technical excellence with real-world impact=
. I've been following NAB's digital transformation journey, particularly yo=
ur move toward modern React architectures and micro-frontends, and I'm exci=
ted about the opportunity to contribute to that evolution.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">I'm particularly passionate abou=
t performance optimization and developer experience. At my current role, I'=
ve spent a lot of time working with advanced React patterns, state manageme=
nt solutions, and most recently, I've been experimenting with the new React=
 Compiler that was just released as RC.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's great to hear you've been exploring React Compiler! That's actually go=
ing to be a significant focus of our conversation today, as we're evaluatin=
g it for adoption across our frontend teams. Before we dive deep into that,=
 I'd love to hear about your experience with state management at scale. You=
 mentioned working with trading platforms - those tend to have pretty compl=
ex state requirements. What's been your approach to managing state in large=
 React applications?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Exce=
llent question! State management at scale is definitely one of the most cri=
tical architectural decisions. In my experience with the trading platform, =
we actually evolved through several approaches as the application grew.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">We started with a <strong>Redux-=
based architecture</strong> using Redux Toolkit, which served us well initi=
ally. For a trading platform, you need to manage real-time market data, use=
r portfolio state, order management, and complex UI states like chart confi=
gurations and watchlists. Redux gave us that predictable state flow and exc=
ellent DevTools experience.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">However, as we scaled to handle =
more real-time data streams, we hit some performance bottlenecks. The issue=
 wasn't really Redux itself, but rather the fact that we were <strong>over-=
normalizing our state</strong> and causing unnecessary re-renders across co=
mponents that didn't actually need to update when market prices changed.</p=
>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">We ended up implementing a <stro=
ng>hybrid approach</strong>:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Zustand</strong> for li=
ghtweight, isolated UI state and user preferences</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>React Query (now TanSta=
ck Query)</strong> for all server state management and real-time data synch=
ronization</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Jotai</strong> for spec=
ific high-frequency updates like live price feeds, because its atomic appro=
ach allowed us to have very granular subscriptions</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key insight was recognizing =
that <strong>server state and client state have fundamentally different cha=
racteristics</strong>. Server state is asynchronous, can become stale, and =
needs cache invalidation strategies. Client state is synchronous and owned =
by the client.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">For the real-time market data sp=
ecifically, we used Jotai atoms with a custom provider that maintained WebS=
ocket connections. This allowed individual components to subscribe only to =
the specific instruments they needed to display, dramatically reducing unne=
cessary re-renders.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's a sophisticated approach, and I appreciate how you've thought about th=
e different characteristics of various state types. The hybrid pattern is s=
omething we've been discussing internally as well. You mentioned performanc=
e bottlenecks with Redux - can you walk me through how you identified and d=
iagnosed those issues? And more specifically, what tools and techniques did=
 you use to measure the impact of your optimizations?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t follow-up question! Performance debugging in React applications, especial=
ly at scale, requires a multi-layered approach. Let me walk you through our=
 methodology:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Identification Phase:</s=
trong>
We started noticing performance issues when users complained about UI lag d=
uring high-volume trading periods. Our first step was instrumenting the app=
lication with <strong>React DevTools Profiler</strong> in production (with =
appropriate safeguards). We discovered that components were re-rendering fa=
r more frequently than necessary - sometimes hundreds of times per second d=
uring market hours.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Measurement Tools:</stro=
ng></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>React DevTools Profiler=
</strong>: Essential for understanding component render frequency and durat=
ion</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance.measure() A=
PI</strong>: We wrapped key operations to measure actual impact</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Custom Redux middleware=
</strong> to track action frequency and payload sizes</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Web Vitals monitoring</=
strong>: Particularly focused on Interaction to Next Paint (INP) since trad=
ing requires immediate feedback</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Here's an example of our custom =
