<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Impact Measurement:</str=
ong></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Reduced average component rende=
r time from <strong>~45ms to ~3ms</strong> for portfolio components</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Decreased total render count du=
ring peak hours by <strong>~70%</strong></li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Improved INP scores from <stron=
g>~300ms to ~120ms</strong></li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Reduced bundle size by <strong>=
~15%</strong> after removing unnecessary memoization wrappers</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The most interesting insight was=
 that <strong>profiling in development wasn't enough</strong> - we had to p=
rofile in production-like conditions with real data volumes and user intera=
ction patterns to understand the true performance characteristics.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent analysis! I can see you have deep experience with performance optim=
ization. Now, this brings us nicely to React Compiler. You mentioned you've=
 been experimenting with it - this is particularly relevant because we're c=
onsidering adopting it across our teams at NAB. Can you explain what React =
Compiler actually does under the hood, and how it would have impacted the p=
erformance issues you just described?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Abso=
lutely! React Compiler represents a <strong>fundamental shift in how we thi=
nk about React optimization</strong> - moving from manual, developer-driven=
 memoization to automatic, compiler-driven optimization.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>What React Compiler Actu=
ally Does:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">At its core, React Compiler is a=
 <strong>build-time transform</strong> that automatically applies memoizati=
on patterns by analyzing your code and understanding the Rules of React. It=
's essentially doing what we used to do manually with <code class=3D"bg-tex=
t-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 whitespace-pre-=
wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">useMemo</code>, <code class=
=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 text-danger-000 white=
space-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]">useCallback</code=
>, and <code class=3D"bg-text-200/5 border border-0.5 border-border-300 tex=
t-danger-000 whitespace-pre-wrap rounded-[0.4rem] px-1 py-px text-[0.9rem]"=
>React.memo</code>, but with much more precision and granularity.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The compiler works through sever=
al phases:</p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Static Analysis</strong=
>: It builds a dependency graph of your components and hooks</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Effect Inference</stron=
g>: It determines which values and functions need to be memoized based on t=
heir dependencies</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Code Generation</strong=
>: It injects the optimal memoization automatically</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>How It Would Have Helped=
 Our Trading Platform:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">Looking back at our performance =
issues, React Compiler would have addressed several problems automatically:=
</p>
