<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">But here's the <strong>key advan=
tage</strong>: The compiler can be <strong>more granular</strong> than manu=
al memoization. Instead of memoizing entire objects, it can memoize individ=
ual properties or even sub-expressions, leading to more efficient updates.<=
/p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Specific Benefits for Ou=
r Use Case:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Reduced Developer Cogni=
tive Load</strong>: No more mental overhead of remembering to memoize</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Fewer Bugs</strong>: El=
iminates incorrect dependency arrays in manual memoization</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Better Granularity</str=
ong>: The compiler can optimize at levels that would be tedious to do manua=
lly</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Consistency</strong>: E=
very developer's code gets the same level of optimization</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Potential Challenges:</s=
trong></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Bundle Size</strong>: I=
nitially might increase due to added memoization code</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Debugging Complexity</s=
trong>: The generated code can be harder to debug</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Build Time</strong>: Ad=
ditional compilation step adds to build duration</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Rule Compliance</strong=
>: Requires strict adherence to Rules of React</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The most exciting aspect is that=
 it would have <strong>eliminated entire classes of performance bugs</stron=
g> we encountered, particularly around accidental object recreation and cal=
lback instability.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Th=
at's a thorough understanding of React Compiler's benefits. You touched on =
something important - "strict adherence to Rules of React." In a large orga=
nization like NAB, we have hundreds of developers working on React codebase=
s, and not everyone might be following the Rules of React perfectly. What s=
pecific Rules of React violations have you encountered in real projects, an=
d how would you handle the adoption of React Compiler in a legacy codebase =
that might have some violations?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's an excellent and very practical question! In my experience, <strong>Rule=
s of React violations are more common than we'd like to admit</strong>, esp=
ecially in legacy codebases or when teams are under pressure to deliver qui=
ckly.</p>
