<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Risk Mitigation:</strong=
></p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>A/B testing</strong>: R=
un compiled and non-compiled versions side by side</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Gradual rollout</strong=
>: Start with low-risk, non-critical components</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Rollback strategy</stro=
ng>: Feature flags to quickly disable compiler if issues arise</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Team training</strong>:=
 Workshops on Rules of React before broader adoption</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key is <strong>not rushing t=
he adoption</strong> and ensuring we have proper measurement and rollback c=
apabilities at every step.</p>
<hr>
<h2 class=3D"text-xl font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-0.5">Part 2: Deep Tec=
hnical Discussion</h2>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: I =
really appreciate the methodical approach you've outlined. That kind of sys=
tematic thinking is exactly what we need for enterprise-level changes. Now,=
 let's dive deeper into React Compiler's internals. Can you explain how the=
 compiler determines what to memoize? And what are some edge cases or limit=
ations you've discovered in your experimentation?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Exce=
llent question! Understanding React Compiler's <strong>static analysis engi=
ne</strong> is crucial for effective adoption, especially when debugging un=
expected behavior.</p>
