<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Production Consideration=
s for NAB:</strong></p>
<ol class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Error Boundaries</stron=
g>: Compiler bugs could cause runtime errors, so robust error boundaries ar=
e essential</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Build Pipeline</strong>=
: Need fallback to non-compiled code if compilation fails</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Performance Monitoring<=
/strong>: Track before/after metrics to validate improvements</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Team Training</strong>:=
 Developers need to understand when/why the compiler bails out</li>
</ol>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The biggest lesson from my exper=
imentation is that <strong>React Compiler works best with clean, idiomatic =
React code</strong>. It's not a magic bullet for poorly architected compone=
nts, but it's incredibly powerful when applied to well-structured codebases=
.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Fa=
scinating insights! Your experience with the edge cases will be valuable as=
 we plan our adoption strategy. Now, I want to shift to a different but rel=
ated topic. At NAB, we have a complex micro-frontend architecture where dif=
ferent teams own different parts of the application. How would you approach=
 introducing React Compiler in a micro-frontend environment? And what consi=
derations would you have around bundle splitting, shared dependencies, and =
potential version conflicts?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's a <strong>fantastic question</strong> that gets to the heart of enterpri=
se React architecture! Micro-frontends add several layers of complexity to =
React Compiler adoption, and I've actually worked through similar challenge=
s in my current role where we have a <strong>federated micro-frontend setup=
</strong>.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Micro-Frontend Architect=
ure Considerations:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>1. Compiler Version Cons=
istency:</strong>
The biggest challenge is ensuring <strong>consistent compilation behavior</=
strong> across micro-frontends. Different teams might adopt React Compiler =
at different times or use different configurations, leading to inconsistent=
 optimization behavior.</p>
