<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Bottom Line for NAB:</st=
rong>
React Compiler is <strong>extremely beneficial</strong> for most trading da=
shboard components, particularly those handling portfolio calculations, for=
matting, and user interactions. However, for the <strong>highest frequency =
price update components</strong>, I'd recommend a hybrid approach where we =
opt out of compilation for maximum performance control while leveraging the=
 compiler for everything else.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">The key is <strong>measuring eve=
rything</strong> and making data-driven decisions about where to use compil=
er optimization versus manual optimization.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Ex=
cellent! Your understanding of the performance trade-offs in high-frequency=
 scenarios is exactly what we need. Now, let's shift to something equally i=
mportant but different - maintainability and team collaboration. At NAB, we=
 have teams of different skill levels working on the same codebase. How wou=
ld React Compiler impact code reviews, mentoring junior developers, and ove=
rall code maintainability? And what would be your strategy for ensuring cod=
e quality remains high when the compiler is doing a lot of the optimization=
 work?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: That=
's a <strong>crucial question</strong> that often gets overlooked when disc=
ussing React Compiler adoption! The impact on team dynamics, code quality, =
and knowledge transfer is just as important as the technical benefits.</p>
